# __AOH_NAME__

__AOH_DESCRIPTION__

A Go microservice in the AOH (Agil Ops Hub) platform. Layered architecture
(handler → service → repo), chi router, sqlx + PostgreSQL, Viper config,
Keycloak/JWT auth via `aoh-golib`.

This service lives inside a Turborepo monorepo. Depending on the monorepo
shape, that's either `apps/<service-name>/` (consumer-app shape) or
`modules/<module-name>/service/` (ops-hub platform-monorepo shape). The
service-internal layout below is the same either way.

## Quick start

From the repo root:

```bash
# Bring up shared platform infrastructure (Traefik, IAMS, SDS, this service's DB).
# In the consumer-app shape this lives at `compose/`; in ops-hub it's the hub stack
# wired via `modules/<name>/deploy/compose.snippet.yml`.
cd compose && docker compose up -d

# Install workspace deps (first time, or after pnpm-lock.yaml changes).
pnpm install

# Run the service.
cd <this-service-directory> && make run
```

Once the stack is healthy, the service is reachable at
`http://__AOH_NAME__.${DEV_DOMAIN}` (Traefik routes by host header). Swagger
UI is at `/swagger-ui/index.html`.

## Observability

This service is **born instrumented** with OpenTelemetry via `aoh-golib/otel`:
every request gets a server span (named by chi route pattern), RED/HTTP +
Go-runtime metrics, and a trace-correlated access log (`trace_id`/`span_id`).
It is a no-op until you point it at a Collector:

```bash
# the per-spoke OTEL gateway (the `otel` aoh-compose fragment)
OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT=http://localhost:4317 make run
```

`service.name` is `__AOH_NAME__`; `service.namespace=aoh` and the W3C
propagators are set automatically. Logs stay on zap (stdout) with trace IDs
injected — no backend lock-in.

> **Container build:** the `Dockerfile` is a standalone single-module build —
> `aoh-golib` is fetched from the private ops-hub repo, so run
> `go mod tidy` first (go.sum must exist) and build from **this service
> directory**. Authenticate the private-module fetch one of two ways:
>
> ```bash
> # 1. Access token in a file (file-sourced secrets work in podman AND docker;
> #    .secrets/ is gitignored — a path outside the repo works too)
> podman build --secret id=access_token,src=.secrets/access_token -t __AOH_NAME__:local -f Dockerfile .
>
> # 2. Spoke registry mirror — no token
> podman build --build-arg GOPROXY=<mirror-url> --build-arg GOSUMDB=off -t __AOH_NAME__:local -f Dockerfile .
> ```
>
> (`docker build` takes the same flags.) The token is mounted only for the
> dependency-download step and never lands in an image layer.

## Layout inside this service

```
├── cmd/server/             # Entry point: main.go (startup/shutdown), init.go (DI wiring)
├── internal/
│   ├── handler/            # HTTP layer — chi sub-routers, swag annotations, BearerAuth
│   ├── service/            # Business logic — interfaces.go, errors.go, per-entity files
│   ├── repo/               # Data access — sqlx queries, per-entity files
│   └── config/             # Viper config (CLI flags > env > YAML > defaults)
├── schema/                 # SQL migrations — golang-migrate NNNN_{title}.up.sql / .down.sql
├── docs/                   # swag-generated swagger.json/yaml/docs.go
├── Makefile                # Build, test, lint, mock, swag, migration targets
└── Dockerfile              # Multi-stage Go build
```

In an ops-hub module, sibling directories at `modules/<module-name>/` (e.g.
`sdk/`, `client/`, `types/`, `deploy/`) wire this service into a published
Svelte SDK, a typed REST client, TS types, and deployment artifacts. See
the module-level `README.md` for the cross-cutting view.

## Build & development commands

From this service's directory:

| Command | Purpose |
|---------|---------|
| `make build` | Build the server binary |
| `make run` | Build and run with debug logging |
| `make lint-code` | Run golangci-lint |
| `make mock` | Regenerate mockery mocks |
| `make swag` | Regenerate Swagger docs (writes `docs/`) |
| `make migrate-create NAME={title}` | Generate numbered migration files in `schema/` |
| `go test ./...` | Run all tests |
| `go test -run TestName ./path/to/package` | Run a single test |

From the repo root via Turbo:

```bash
turbo run build --filter=__AOH_NAME__
turbo run test --filter=__AOH_NAME__
turbo run dev --filter=__AOH_NAME__
```

## Authentication

All entity endpoints require a valid Keycloak Bearer token. The auth middleware
stack runs `aohhttp.BearerAuth` (validates JWT against Keycloak's userinfo
endpoint), then `withUserInfo` (extracts JWT claims into request context).

Health endpoints (`/livez`, `/readyz`) and Swagger UI (`/swagger-ui/`) are
unauthenticated.

IAMS configuration (in `config.yaml` or env vars):

| Key | Env var | Default |
|-----|---------|---------|
| `iams.keycloak_host` | `IAMS_KEYCLOAK_HOST` | `http://iams-keycloak` |
| `iams.keycloak_port` | `IAMS_KEYCLOAK_PORT` | `8080` |
| `iams.keycloak_realm` | `IAMS_KEYCLOAK_REALM` | `aoh` |

When testing with cURL or Postman, request tokens with `scope=openid` — the
userinfo endpoint returns 403 without it. See the `aoh-conventions` skill's
`references/go.md` → "Testing with cURL" for the full incantation.

